{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>May YHWH lift His face toward you.</b> In tractate Rosh HaShanah (17b) they raised the difficulty: it is written (Deuteronomy 10:17), ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִשָּׂא פָנִים״, and it is written ״יִשָּׂא ה׳ פָּנָיו אֵלֶיךָ״, etc. And the Tosafot wrote that this is no difficulty, for here it speaks of the face of the Holy One, blessed be He, while there it speaks of the face of man — that HaShem does not show favoritism to a great man. And it appears that the opinion of the one who raises the difficulty is that every lifting of faces refers to faces that are liable to fall or to be subdued, and they are lifted by way of forbearance, as it is said (Genesis 4:7–8): ״לָמָּה נָפְלוּ פָנֶיךָ, הֲלֹא אִם תֵּיטִיב שְׂאֵת״ — they will be lifted. And when it says there ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִשָּׂא פָנִים״, this means that the face of the sinner that is liable to fall — like the face of Cain and his companions — HaShem will not lift them to raise them from their fall; rather, the one who is liable to fall shall fall, ״דֹּחוּ וְלֹא יָכְלוּ קוּם״ (Psalms 36:13), for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not forgive at all. And here it says ״יִשָּׂא ה׳ פָּנָיו אֵלֶיךָ״ — since it says ״יִשָּׂא״, infer from this that even at the time of anger, when Israel are liable to be in a state of hiddenness of face, and whoever hides his face is as though they are subdued below, as it were — even so, the Holy One, blessed be He, says: by way of forbearance I will lift My face to you, so that they will be directed toward your face. For one says ״יִשָּׂא״ only regarding one who is liable to fall or liable to be subdued. Difficult? Forbearance — and even more forbearance!" "And in the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah 11:7) the Holy One, blessed be He, said: And how could I not show them favor? I said in the Torah (Deuteronomy 8:10), ״וְאָכַלְתָּ וְשָׂבָעְתָּ וּבֵרַכְתָּ״, and they are stringent even to the size of an olive and even to the size of an egg. That is to say: behold, I took a blessing from them — a blessing! — and shall I not return it? And how could I not return to them a blessing by way of forbearance, in exchange for the blessing that I am also taking from them by way of forbearance? And what is written, ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִשָּׂא פָנִים״ — that refers to all the nations, who do nothing at all by way of forbearance; but here it says specifically ״יִשָּׂא ה׳ פָּנָיו אֵלֶיךָ״, for you act by way of forbearance and are stringent upon yourselves — therefore I too will lift My face toward you by way of forbearance. And even this forbearance is not in every matter, but is measure for measure, literally: for you are stringent to the size of an olive even without satiation, as though you were sated — so too I will forbear for you, that even one who eats a mere morsel is blessed in his innards. And of this it says ״וְיָשֵׂם לְךָ שָׁלוֹם״, as it is written (Psalms 147:14): ״הַשָּׂם גְּבוּלֵךְ שָׁלוֹם, חֵלֶב חִטִּים יַשְׂבִּיעֵךְ״. And so too He says (Leviticus 26:5–6): ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם לַחְמְכֶם לָשׂבַע וִישַׁבְתֶּם לָבֶטַח בְּאַרְצְכֶם, וְנָתַתִּי שָׁלוֹם בָּאָרֶץ״, and this explanation is correct and clear in the understanding of this Midrash."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The closing phrase 'קַשְׁיָא וִתּוּר אַוִּתּוּר' is rendered hyper-literally as a rhyming play on words: 'Difficult? Forbearance — and even more forbearance!' The phrase appears to be a formulaic flourish pairing the difficulty (קושיא) with the resolution (ויתור, forbearance/concession), possibly with a punning repetition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:37.634806746Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}